EXAMPLE 2.5
(c) Suppose that the same system of charges is now placed in an external electric field E = A(1/r^2); A = 9 \times 10^5 \,\mathrm{NC^{-1}} m<sup>2</sup>. What would the electrostatic energy of the configuration be?
Solution
(a) U = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \frac{q_1 q_2}{r} = 9 \times 10^9 \times \frac{7 \times (-2) \times 10^{-12}}{0.18} = -0.7 \text{ J}.
(b) W = U_2 - U_1 = 0 - U = 0 - (-0.7) = 0.7 \text{ J}.
(c) The mutual interaction energy of the two charges remains unchanged. In addition, there is the energy of interaction of the two charges with the external electric field. We find,
q_1V(\mathbf{r}_1) + q_2V(\mathbf{r}_2) = A\frac{7\mu\text{C}}{0.09\text{m}} + A\frac{-2\mu\text{C}}{0.09\text{m}}
